# Copy to .env.local (never commit real secrets).

# --- Web ---
NODE_ENV=development
PORT=3000

# --- Claude / Agent runtime (Thing 2) ---
# Where Claude runs (docs/client-deployment.md): anthropic (default) | bedrock | vertex.
# Anything else stops every run — it never falls back to another provider.
# HUB_MODEL_PROVIDER=anthropic
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# bedrock: AWS_REGION is required (e.g. eu-central-1); credentials from the AWS chain
# or AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK. Every model in use must be mapped in HUB_MODEL_IDS.
# AWS_REGION=eu-central-1
# vertex: region and project; credentials are Google's application default credentials.
# CLOUD_ML_REGION=europe-west1
# ANTHROPIC_VERTEX_PROJECT_ID=
# The hub's model names → the provider's, as JSON (required on Bedrock).
# HUB_MODEL_IDS={"claude-opus-4-8":"eu.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8-v1"}
# Every run is told today's date and time in this zone (IANA name). Unset ⇒ the
# server's own zone, which on Fly is UTC — so a Danish team should set it.
# HUB_TIMEZONE=Europe/Copenhagen

# How much of the hub people see until they choose for themselves (docs/views.md):
# simple | standard | expert. Unset ⇒ by role: admin → expert, analyst → standard,
# viewer → simple. A view hides and never grants; roles still decide what anyone
# may do. A client firm whose people mostly ask and approve: set simple.
# HUB_DEFAULT_VIEW=simple

# The key connections added on the Connections page are encrypted with
# (docs/connections.md): at least 32 characters, e.g. `openssl rand -base64 48`.
# Unset ⇒ derived from WORKOS_COOKIE_PASSWORD, which works until that is rotated.
# HUB_SECRET_KEY=

# --- LLM observability + ROI data (Thing 2) ---
# LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY=
# LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY=
# LANGFUSE_HOST=https://cloud.langfuse.com
# Traces carry names, sizes, tokens and timings — never prompts, messages, tool
# inputs/outputs or answers — unless this is 1 (docs/data-protection-review.md,
# 2.6). Turn it on only for a Langfuse you are happy holding client content.
# HUB_TRACE_CONTENT=1

# --- A client's own hub (docs/client-deployment.md) ---
# The vendor: addresses or domains that get in only through temporary access an
# admin grants (Admin → Temporary access), never standing access. Set it on
# every client's hub; leave it unset on the vendor's own.
# HUB_VENDOR_ACCESS=teqneo.co
# Which seeded agents each release installs: unset = all (the vendor's own hub),
# none, or slugs, e.g. context-steward. A slug that is not a seed stops the release.
# HUB_SEEDS=context-steward
# Check a deployment's settings before it ships: npm run deploy:check -w web

# --- Retention (docs/retention.md) ---
# How long the hub keeps what people asked and were answered, in whole days
# (0–3650; anything else falls back to the default). After these only the
# numbers stay. Swept every six hours where HUB_SCHEDULER=1.
# A run's full transcript — every message and tool result. Default 7.
# HUB_RETAIN_TRANSCRIPTS_DAYS=7
# A run's question, answer and error; a decided proposal's details. Default 90.
# HUB_RETAIN_ANSWERS_DAYS=90
# The audit trail (records of erasure are kept). Default 365.
# HUB_RETAIN_AUDIT_DAYS=365

# --- Database: Postgres + pgvector (Thing 4/5) ---
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/aihub

# --- Embeddings (Thing 5) ---
# VOYAGE_API_KEY=

# --- Remote MCP servers (Thing 3) ---
# Client systems that expose their own MCP endpoint over Streamable HTTP. Unlike
# the built-in servers (config in code), these are deployment-specific, so they
# are declared here and auto-registered into the control plane on first read.
# `tokenEnv` names another env var holding the bearer token — keep the secret out
# of the JSON blob. Malformed JSON ⇒ no remote servers (fails closed).
#
# The same endpoint is declared TWICE on purpose, under two names holding two
# credentials. PM-tool omits its write tools from the tool list for a token
# without the `write` scope (lib/mcp/tools.ts), so which token an agent holds
# decides what it is physically able to do:
#
#   pm-tool       read+write  the project manager, who is meant to change things
#   pm-tool-read  read only   the product owners, who read the board and never change it
#
# That is what makes "the secretary cannot write" a property of the credential
# rather than a sentence in a prompt.
#
# When a PERSON is behind the call (they signed in to the hub by OAuth), an
# entry marked `"forwardIdentity": true` is dialled with THEIR token instead of
# its own, so the PM-tool sees them — and would offer them their writes. An
# entry marked `"readOnly": true` (the default for a name ending in `-read`)
# then keeps only the tools the PM-tool annotates as read-only. Forwarding is
# off unless set: the person's token is valid at the hub itself, so it goes
# only to servers we vouch for. See docs/auth.md, "Signing in once". Mint the read-scoped token in PM-tool's
# PM_TOOL_MCP_TOKENS with its own label — the audit trail is attributed to that
# label, so it must not borrow a person's token. See docs/secretary-agent.md.
#
# An agent naming a server this file does not declare is dropped SILENTLY at run
# time (runSession filters unknown names, the same way /admin disables one), so
# the secretary would run with memory alone and ground its change set on
# nothing. `npm run seed:secretary -w @ai-hub/db` warns when that is the case.
# HUB_REMOTE_MCP_SERVERS={"pm-tool":{"url":"http://localhost:3000/api/mcp","label":"PM-tool","description":"Project delivery data: projects, tasks, risks, status. Read + write.","tokenEnv":"PM_TOOL_MCP_TOKEN","forwardIdentity":true},"pm-tool-read":{"url":"http://localhost:3000/api/mcp","label":"PM-tool (read-only)","description":"Project delivery data, read-only: the secretary proposes changes and never applies them.","tokenEnv":"PM_TOOL_READ_TOKEN","forwardIdentity":true}}
# PM_TOOL_MCP_TOKEN=
# PM_TOOL_READ_TOKEN=
#
# GitHub, read-only, through GitHub's own remote MCP server — so an agent that
# owns a product can read the repository live: files, commits, pull requests,
# issues. The /readonly endpoint offers no write tools, the X-MCP-Toolsets
# header keeps the tool list to the three toolsets that matter, and the same
# fine-grained token serves the docs source below. See docs/github.md.
#   "github":{"url":"https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/readonly","label":"GitHub","description":"The repositories we build, read-only: files, commits, pull requests, issues.","tokenEnv":"GITHUB_TOKEN","headers":{"X-MCP-Toolsets":"repos,issues,pull_requests"},"readOnly":true}

# --- GitHub: the design record of the products we build ---
# A fine-grained personal access token with Contents: read (and Issues,
# Pull requests: read for the MCP server above) on the listed repositories
# only. Used by the `github` MCP entry and by the docs source. Never a classic
# token: that reads every repository the person can.
# GITHUB_TOKEN=
# One knowledge source per repository (type github:<key>): the prose under
# `paths` is indexed at one commit and filed under `collection`. Malformed JSON
# ⇒ no sources. Sync from /knowledge → Sources. See docs/github.md.
# HUB_GITHUB_SOURCES={"hub":{"repo":"thor1033/ai-consultancy-hub","ref":"master","paths":["docs","README.md","CLAUDE.md"],"collection":"hub-product"},"atlas":{"repo":"thor1033/pm-tool","ref":"main","paths":["README.md","AGENTS.md","PARITY-NOTES.md"],"collection":"atlas-product"}}

# --- Microsoft 365: the secretary agent's mailbox and calendar ---
# Read-only Mail.Read + Calendars.Read via an app-only (client credentials) app
# registration with admin consent. No user sign-in, no refresh token to rotate.
#
# Application permissions reach EVERY mailbox in the tenant, so scope the
# registration to the secretary alone with an Application Access Policy or this
# secret reads the whole organisation's mail. See docs/secretary-agent.md.
#
# Missing any of the four ⇒ the server exits 1 naming what is missing, and the
# agent run fails loudly rather than reporting an empty inbox.
# M365_TENANT_ID=
# M365_CLIENT_ID=
# M365_CLIENT_SECRET=
# The DEFAULT mailbox. An agent names its own on its server entry
# ({"name":"microsoft365","mailbox":"…"}, set under Settings → What it may use),
# so one deployment serves several agents, each reading one shared mailbox
# inside the access policy's group.
# M365_MAILBOX=secretary@yourdomain.com
# Times are rendered in this zone (Windows or IANA name). Defaults to UTC.
# M365_TIMEZONE=Europe/Copenhagen

# --- Microsoft Teams: meeting transcripts and the team's files ---
# Read-only, on the same app registration as above, with Application
# permissions OnlineMeetings.Read.All, OnlineMeetingTranscript.Read.All and
# Sites.Selected. Each half is fenced by Microsoft: a Teams application access
# policy granted to the organiser, and a Sites.Selected read grant on the site.
# See docs/teams.md.
#
# Each half switches on by itself. Neither set ⇒ the server refuses to start.
# The organiser's Entra OBJECT ID (a GUID), not their email address.
# M365_TEAMS_ORGANIZER_ID=
# The team's SharePoint site (Teams → Files → Open in SharePoint).
# M365_TEAMS_SITE_URL=https://yourtenant.sharepoint.com/sites/YourTeam
# Channel meetings only, unless this is exactly 1: the organiser's private
# meetings would otherwise be readable by anyone who can run the agent.
# M365_TEAMS_PRIVATE_MEETINGS=
# Where channel meetings are found: Microsoft's bulk transcript listing leaves
# them out, so the server reads this mailbox's calendar for Teams join links.
# Defaults to M365_MAILBOX. Invite that mailbox to the team's meeting series.
# M365_TEAMS_CALENDAR=
# Where the `teams-notes` server saves meeting notes, inside the team's files.
# Needs the organiser, the site, and a Sites.Selected WRITE grant. Unset ⇒
# teams-notes refuses to start; the read-only `teams` server never writes.
# M365_TEAMS_NOTES_FOLDER=Meetings/Notes

# --- The Context Steward (docs/context-steward.md) ---
# Whose chats the steward may read is each person's own switch, in Settings
# ("Context Steward"). HUB_STEWARD_READERS is no longer read.
# How it files and searches: the hub's own API, as its own credential (issue one
# in /admin with the analyst role). Without these, find and file say so.
# HUB_STEWARD_TOKEN=
# Where meeting notes are filed; the one collection it reads in full. Default: meetings.
# HUB_STEWARD_NOTES_COLLECTION=meetings
# Which runs are handed to the steward is not an environment setting: the
# steward's seed declares two hand-off rows (the Secretary's meeting skills),
# shown and switchable on the steward's Hand-offs tab. docs/agent-patterns.md.

# --- The scheduler (unattended agent runs) ---
# Off unless set to exactly 1. The consequence of being wrong is an agent acting
# unbidden — a dev container pointed at production's database should not start
# reading someone's mailbox every minute — so production opts in rather than
# development opting out. Safe on several Fly machines: they all tick, and the
# claim in agent_schedules decides which one wins. See docs/schedules.md.
# HUB_SCHEDULER=1

# --- Security / AuthZ ---
# OAuth 2.1, with WorkOS AuthKit as the authorization server. This is the front
# door: it is what lets ChatGPT Enterprise and M365 Copilot enrol the hub as a
# connector, and it is what makes a person a revocable row rather than a secret.
# See docs/auth.md.
#
# The AuthKit origin, which is also the token issuer. Unset ⇒ OAuth is off and
# the hub is reachable by credential only (no enterprise surface can enrol it).
# WORKOS_AUTHKIT_DOMAIN=https://your-app.authkit.app
#
# This server's RFC 8707 resource indicator: the value clients request and the
# `aud` we require. Must match the Resource Indicator configured in the WorkOS
# dashboard exactly. Defaults to <HUB_BASE_URL>/api/mcp.
# HUB_MCP_RESOURCE=https://hub.example.com/api/mcp
#
# Public origin, used for redirects and to derive the resource indicator.
# HUB_BASE_URL=https://hub.example.com
#
# Email domains whose people are let in on their first OAuth sign-in, as
# `analyst`, instead of bouncing until an admin makes them a row. Off unless
# set. Admin is never granted this way. See docs/auth.md, "Signing in once".
# A first sign-in needs WORKOS_API_KEY (already required by the web sign-in):
# the access token names the person only by id, and the hub asks WorkOS for
# the email once, then binds the id to the row.
# HUB_AUTO_PROVISION_DOMAINS=teqneo.co
#
# Who may sign in to the browser UI at all, comma-separated. REQUIRED: unset or
# empty lets nobody in — there is no built-in list. Being listed is not access:
# a person also needs a row (made on /admin, or by HUB_AUTO_PROVISION_DOMAINS).
# The one exception is an empty hub: its first listed person to sign in becomes
# its admin, once, on the audit trail. See docs/auth.md.
# ALLOWED_EMAILS=you@yourcompany.com,colleague@yourcompany.com

# --- Knowledge access (increment 02) ---
# How a collection nobody has classified (and an unfiled document) is treated.
# `restricted` (the default) means nothing is readable without an explicit grant
# in /admin → Knowledge; admins read everything. `open` makes the unclassified
# readable by everyone signed in — for a deployment still classifying an older
# corpus. See docs/knowledge-access.md.
# HUB_DEFAULT_COLLECTION_VISIBILITY=open

# --- Security / AuthZ: the deprecated token map -----------------------------
# REPLACED by per-principal credentials issued from /admin (`hub_sk_…`), which
# are revocable without a deploy and leave an audit trail. This map is none of
# those things: each deploy replaces it wholesale, which on 6 September 2026
# revoked three live tokens in turn and left an .env.example placeholder
# granting admin on a public endpoint for ~31 minutes.
#
# It still resolves so a running deployment keeps working mid-migration. Once
# every client holds a credential, set HUB_DISABLE_LEGACY_TOKENS=1, confirm
# nothing broke, then delete the secret.
# HUB_API_TOKENS={"<random-32-bytes>":{"id":"admin","roles":["admin"]}}
# HUB_DISABLE_LEGACY_TOKENS=1
